{"date":"2026-09-23","heading":"September 23, 2026","editions":[{"edition":"recap","byline":"Yesterday in review","text":"Feeder traffic reached 421 visits, double the recent mean of 213.3. Tufted Titmice accounted for 202 of these visits, while House Finches and Northern Cardinals remained consistent regulars. The species count rose to 23, exceeding the average of 20.1, driven by the return of the Cedar Waxwing, which had been absent since September 15.\n\nVocal activity totaled 251 detections, below the weekly average of 296.4. Carolina Wrens and Eastern Towhees maintained their usual presence, with the former leading in detection count. Eastern Phoebes, recorded every day in the past week, did not appear, nor did Downy Woodpeckers or White-eyed Vireos.\n\nThe day spanned from a Tufted Titmouse seen six minutes before sunrise to a Northern Cardinal seven minutes after sunset.","time":"06:52"},{"edition":"morning","byline":"Dawn patrol","text":"A Gray Catbird touched the feeder at 07:17, four minutes before sunrise, making it the first visual record at this station today. It was immediately joined by Tufted Titmice, who dominated the early morning activity with 73 confident visits.\n\nVocal activity totaled 128 detections by 10:35. Northern Cardinals led the heard detections with 25, followed by Carolina Chickadees with 29. Eastern Towhees also registered 18 detections.\n\nThe feeder had 153 visits so far. House Finches and Mourning Doves made up the bulk of the other visitors, with six confident visits each. Tufted Titmice continued to be the most frequent visual visitors even as the species count reached 18.","time":"10:35"}],"sunrise":"07:20","sunset":"19:26","weather":{"high_c":17.222,"wind_kph":1.0460999999999998,"precip_mm":0.0,"hourly":["partly","cloudy","cloudy","partly","clear","partly","cloudy","partly","cloudy","cloudy",null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null]},"counts":{"species":18,"heard":13,"seen":12,"both":7,"visits":15